{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.064,
            0.378,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.12,
            0.886,
            0.188
          ],
          "content": "Z, P and T. These points are read as ‘Point Z’, ‘Point P’ and ‘Point T’. Of course, the dots represent precise locations and must be imagined to be invisibly thin."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.17,
            0.216,
            0.422,
            0.241
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.2 Line Segment"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.255,
            0.645,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": "Fold a piece of paper and unfold it. Do you see a crease? This gives the idea of a line segment. It has two end points, A and B."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.324,
            0.646,
            0.391
          ],
          "content": "Mark any two points A and B on a sheet of paper. Try to connect A to B by various routes (Fig. 2.1)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.392,
            0.648,
            0.53
          ],
          "content": "What is the shortest route from A to B? This shortest path from point A to Point B (including A and B) as shown here is called the **line segment** from A to B. It is denoted by either AB or BA. The points A and B are called the **end points** of the line segment AB."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.655,
            0.243,
            0.88,
            0.497
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.756,
                0.499,
                0.82,
                0.517
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 2.1"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.17,
            0.555,
            0.292,
            0.58
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.3 Line"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.596,
            0.66,
            0.731
          ],
          "content": "Imagine that the line segment from A to B (i.e., AB) is extended beyond A in one direction and beyond B in the other direction without any end (see Fig. 2.2). This is a model for a **line.** Do you think you can draw a complete picture of a line? No. Why?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.678,
            0.581,
            0.884,
            0.71
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_1_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.731,
            0.885,
            0.799
          ],
          "content": "A line through two points A and B is written as $\\overrightarrow { \\mathrm { A B } }$ . It extends forever in both directions. Sometimes a line is denoted by a letter like l or m."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.801,
            0.885,
            0.846
          ],
          "content": "Observe that any two points determine a unique line that passes through both of them."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.086,
            0.941,
            0.113,
            0.96
          ],
          "content": "14"
        },
